    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-lanka-kanda-0012",
      "sequence": 867,
      "meaning": "The moon appears as a lion roaming the sky-forest, bursting the dark elephant’s temple and scattering pearl-like stars as Night’s adornment. Rāma asks each friend to explain its dark mark. Sugrīva calls it Earth’s shadow; another says Rāhu struck the moon; another says the Creator took its finest substance for Rati’s face, leaving a hollow through which sky-shadow shows. Rāma says the moon housed its beloved brother poison in its heart and burns separated lovers with poisonous rays. Hanumān says instead that the moon is Rāma’s beloved servant: the dark appearance is the Lord’s image dwelling in its heart. Rāma smiles and turns south.",
